<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Vetulonia</h1><p class="st-meta">850 BCE – 280 BCE (approx.) · capital Vetulonia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Early Etruscan power in the metal-rich hills of northern Etruria, which Roman tradition credited as the source of the fasces and other symbols of office.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Vetulonia, Etruscan Vatluna, grew on a hill overlooking the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-prile">Lake Prile</a>, then a lagoon open to the sea, in the 9th and 8th centuries BCE. Its wealth came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colline-metallifere">Colline Metallifere</a>, and it was one of the leading Etruscan centres of the 8th and 7th centuries BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Rich 'circle tombs' and great tumuli such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietrera-tumulus">Pietrera</a> reveal an aristocracy enriched by metals and trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Its goldsmiths excelled in granulation. The '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomb-of-the-lictor">Tomb of the Lictor</a>' contained an iron double axe with rods, and the Roman poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silius-italicus">Silius Italicus</a> wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> borrowed the fasces, curule chair and purple-bordered toga from Vetulonia.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Vetulonia declined from the 6th century BCE as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/populonia">Populonia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rusellae">Rusellae</a> grew. It revived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>, minting coins, and passed under Roman control in the early 3rd century BCE, later becoming a municipium. Its exact location was only confirmed in the 19th century; the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonna-family">Colonna</a> di Buriano took the ancient name in 1887.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/vetulonia.jpg" alt="Vetulonia" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Trapanator at it.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 2.5 it · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PanoramaVetulonia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>850 BCE – 280 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vetulonia</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Etruscan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Etruscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vatluna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-565&amp;focus=vetulonia" aria-label="Vetulonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4945 655 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Vetulonia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5265 911L5290 918M5265 911L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5290 918h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5265 911h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5265 911h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-565&amp;focus=vetulonia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

